premises.content:
A Slater–Koster tight-binding Hamiltonian on the orthogonal orbital basis $\{\,|p_z^C\rangle,\ |p_z^N\rangle,\ |d_{xz}\rangle,\ |d_{yz}\rangle\,\}\otimes\{|\uparrow\rangle,|\downarrow\rangle\}$,
      $$
      \begin{aligned}
      H_{\mathrm{TB}} &= \sum_{i\alpha}\epsilon_{\alpha}\,c_{i\alpha}^{\dagger}c_{i\alpha}
      -\sum_{\langle i,j\rangle} t_{ij,\alpha\beta}\,c_{i\alpha}^{\dagger}c_{j\beta}
      +t_{\mathrm{SO}}\sum_{i\alpha\beta} c_{i\alpha}^{\dagger}\,\boldsymbol{l}\cdot\boldsymbol{s}\,c_{i\beta},
      \end{aligned}
      $$
      quantitatively reproduces the first-principles band structure of PtN₄C₁₀ near the Dirac points when fitted with the Slater–Koster parameters (in eV): $\epsilon_{p_z}^C=0.15$, $\epsilon_{p_z}^N=-1.17$, $\epsilon_{d_{xz}}^{Pt}=2.35$, $\epsilon_{d_{yz}}^{Pt}=6.52$, $V_{pp\pi}^{CC}=-5.60$, $V_{pp\pi}^{CN}=2.70$, $V_{pd\pi}^{NPt}=1.15$, and $t_{\mathrm{SO}}=1.53$. In this Hamiltonian $c_{i\alpha}^{\dagger}=(c_{i\alpha\uparrow}^{\dagger},c_{i\alpha\downarrow}^{\dagger})$ creates an electron on site $i$ orbital $\alpha$ with spin, $t_{ij,\alpha\beta}$ are nearest-neighbor hoppings determined from Slater–Koster integrals and distance-dependent scaling, $\epsilon_{\alpha}$ are on-site energies, $\boldsymbol{l}$ is the orbital-angular-momentum operator acting in the $d$-orbital subspace, $\boldsymbol{s}$ is the vector of Pauli spin matrices. When $t_{\mathrm{SO}}$ is set to zero the model gives the gapless distorted Dirac cones; when $t_{\mathrm{SO}}\neq0$ the model opens the topological gap, demonstrating that the atomic SOC of Pt $d$ orbitals is the key microscopic mechanism for gap formation.
